    {
      "title": "Mustn't and don't have to",
      "description": "Distinguish prohibition from the absence of obligation.",
      "objective": "Distinguish prohibition from the absence of obligation.",
      "content": "Mustn't means an action is prohibited. Don't have to means an action is optional or unnecessary. They do not have the same meaning.",
      "examples": [
        "You mustn't touch that switch.",
        "You don't have to come early."
      ],
      "commonErrors": [
        "You don't have to park here. -> You mustn't park here for a prohibition.",
        "He doesn't have to wears a tie. -> He doesn't have to wear a tie."
      ],
      "guidedPractice": "Decide whether each situation says no or not necessary before choosing mustn't or don't have to.",
      "summary": "Mustn't means do not do it; don't have to means you may choose not to do it.",
